################ issue astral-sh/ty#4570
#4570 [OPEN] Protocol method implemented by an instance callable is considered abstract
author: jelle-openai  created: 2026-09-21T21:24:06Z  closed: null
labels: needs-decision,protocols
--- body
### Summary

A concrete subclass assigns a callable to an instance attribute with the same name as a protocol method. ty 0.0.81 accepts this; ty 0.0.82 reports `call-non-callable` when constructing the subclass.

```python
from collections.abc import Callable
from typing import Protocol

class Capybara(Protocol):
    def forage(self) -> None: ...

class Hutia(Capybara):
    forage: Callable[[], None]

    def __init__(self) -> None:
        self.forage = lambda: None

Hutia().forage()
```

Expected: no diagnostic. The assignment implements the protocol method, and `Hutia().forage()` executes successfully in Python.

Actual with 0.0.82:

```text
error[call-non-callable]: Cannot instantiate `Hutia` with unimplemented abstract method `forage`
```

0.0.81: `All checks passed!`

The same behavior occurs with an async protocol method and an `AsyncMock`-typed instance attribute assigned in `__init__`.

Possibly related to [#4552](https://github.com/astral-sh/ty/issues/4552), but this example has no slots or abstract property. That issue is closed following a slots-specific fix.


### Version

_No response_
--- comments (3)
[AlexWaygood 2026-09-21] This class can be instantiated:

```pycon
>>> from collections.abc import Callable
... from typing import Protocol
...
... class Capybara(Protocol):
...     def forage(self) -> None: ...
...
... class Hutia(Capybara):
...     forage: Callable[[], None]
...
...     def __init__(self) -> None:
...         self.forage = lambda: None
...
... Hutia().forage()
...
>>>
```

But if you explicitly decorate the protocol method with `@abstractmethod`, it cannot:

```pycon
>>> from collections.abc import Callable
... from typing import Protocol
... from abc import abstractmethod
...
... class Capybara(Protocol):
...     @abstractmethod
...     def forage(self) -> None: ...
...
... class Hutia(Capybara):
---
[AlexWaygood 2026-09-21] There's also the issue that certain protocol methods _cannot_ be soundly implemented by instance attributes (any dunder method that must be looked up on the class rather than the instance, for example)
---
[carljm 2026-09-21] All other type checkers avoid this false positive by generally allowing instance attributes to implement abstract methods; of course this means they instead have false negatives in some cases that will raise at runtime.
